ncy":true,"_score_roic_consistency":77.9,"_gate_incr_roic":0.07607148719066992,"_gp_incr_roic":false,"_score_incr_roic":42.0,"_gate_margin_advantage":0.09365973958377471,"_gp_margin_advantage":true,"_score_margin_advantage":64.6,"_gate_pool_share":null,"_gp_pool_share":null,"_score_pool_share":null,"_gate_rev_durability":null,"_gp_rev_durability":null,"_score_rev_durability":0.0,"_gate_fcf_durability":null,"_gp_fcf_durability":null,"_score_fcf_durability":0.0,"_gate_margins":0.018103495169210698,"_gp_margins":true,"_score_margins":68.1,"_gate_fund_growth":0.16967445036932471,"_gp_fund_growth":true,"_score_fund_growth":100.0,"_gate_shrhldr_yield":0.004210551062150268,"_gp_shrhldr_yield":false,"_score_shrhldr_yield":15.8,"_gate_share_shrink":null,"_gp_share_shrink":null,"_score_share_shrink":0.0,"_gate_sbc_dilution":0.011800433839479393,"_gp_sbc_dilution":true,"_score_sbc_dilution":80.3,"_gate_insider_own":0.14452,"_gp_insider_own":true,"_score_insider_own":96.3,"_gate_insider_buying":0.2,"_gp_insider_buying":false,"_score_insider_buying":20.0,"pf":[],"description_full":"Crane Company, together with its subsidiaries, engages in the manufacture and sale of engineered industrial products in the United States, Canada, the United Kingdom, Continental Europe, and internationally. The company operates in two segments, Aerospace & Advanced Technologies and Process Flow Technologies. The Aerospace & Electronics segment supplies critical components and systems, including original equipment and aftermarket parts for commercial aerospace, as well as the military aerospace, defense, and space markets. This segment also offers pressure sensors for aircraft engine control, aircraft braking systems for commercial aircraft and fighter jets, power conversion solutions for defense, and space applications and lubrication systems. The Process Flow Technologies segment provides process valves and related products, pumps and systems, and commercial valves; valve positioning and control systems, vacuum insulated pipe systems, and valve diagnostic and calibration systems; pumps and systems; and commercial valves. The company was formerly known as Crane Holdings, Co. Crane Company was founded in 1855 and is based in Stamford, Connecticut.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Alejandro A. Alcala | President, CEO & Director | Age 50 | Compensation: $1.5M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 8K people. Revenue per employee of $278K ranks in the below the sector median among Industrials peers, with revenue growing 5%/yr across its filed history. Each employee generates $41K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $3K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 5\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags low compensation governance risk (2/10).","financial_summary":["Crane Company is a large-cap industrials business with a $12.6B market cap on $2.3B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +5% over the past three years, a respectable pace for a business of this size. Operating margins are expanding by +2.4pp year-over-year, which is one of the strongest signals that the moat is deepening.","Returns on capital run at 17% against a 9% cost of capital, a 8-point spread that creates value, though narrow enough that competitive pressure could close it, with gross margins sitting at 41%.","The business throws off $341M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 15 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 1.08\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, leverage is comfortably conservative at 1.4\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and interest coverage of 38\u00d7 is fortress-like.","Shareholder yield is only 0.4%, a thin return of capital, and insiders hold 14% of the shares, so management thinks like owners because it is one \u2014 the strongest alignment there is, and within its sector the business captures 1.6\u00d7 its fair share of profits, a structural advantage visible in the numbers."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Price sits 94% above estimated fair value \u2014 no margin of safety left, and the cushion against estimate error has been bid away (confirmed by 2 other valuation measures)","sev":"red","cat":"valuation","magnitude":0.935235187207488},{"text":"Piotroski score 4/9 \u2014 below the median for a healthy business","sev":"amber","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Insiders are net sellers (80% sell ratio) over the past year","sev":"amber","cat":"insider","ratio":0.2},{"text":"Cyclicality and capital intensity \u2014 order books turn on a dime, and the heavy fixed cost base that creates the moat also magnifies the downturn.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Supply chain reshoring costs and labour shortages \u2014 onshoring mandates raise capex requirements while a shrinking skilled-labour pool inflates operating costs.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Tariff and trade-policy exposure \u2014 industrial exporters sit squarely in the crosshairs of protectionist policy, and retaliatory tariffs can close markets without warning.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Reinvestment rate supports 17% organic growth capacity \u2014 capital is compounding inside the business","Operating margins 9% above sector median \u2014 the quantitative trace of pricing power","Returns on capital have been remarkably steady for 5 years \u2014 durability is the real test of a moat","Altman Z comfortably in the safe zone \u2014 a fortress balance sheet on this measure","Healthy payout ratio leaves room for both dividend growth and reinvestment"],"news_headlines":[{"title":"Trading the Move, Not the Narrative: (CR) Edition","source":"news.stocktradersdaily.com","link":"https://news.stocktradersdaily.com/news_release/90/Trading_the_Move,_Not_the_Narrative:_CR_Edition_112526035002_1795596602.html","date":"2026-11-25","